<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Xin dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">9 CE – 23 CE · capital Chang'an</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The single-reign dynasty of Wang Mang, the Han regent who seized the throne in 9 CE to remake China on ancient models and died as his capital burned.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a> rose through his aunt, the Han empress dowager <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-zhengjun">Wang Zhengjun</a>, and became regent for child emperors of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a>. Presenting himself as a Confucian sage and citing omens, he accepted the abdication of the last infant ruler and proclaimed the Xin, 'New', dynasty in 9 CE, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a> tried to restore the institutions described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rites-of-zhou">Rites of Zhou</a>. He declared all land the king's land, banned the sale of land and slaves, created state monopolies and price controls, and repeatedly changed the coinage, reviving knife-shaped coins. The reforms disrupted the economy, and shifts in the course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> around 11 CE brought flooding and famine.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Peasant risings such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-eyebrows">Red Eyebrows</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulin">Lülin</a> spread. In 23 rebels broke into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Liu Xiu</a>, a Han kinsman, restored the dynasty in 25 as Emperor Guangwu, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/xin-dynasty.jpg" alt="Xin dynasty" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xin_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>9 CE – 23 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chang'an</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wang Mang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Confucian state ritual and ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=16&amp;focus=xin-dynasty" aria-label="Xin dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7449 935 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Xin dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7769 1191L7769 1191"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7769 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chang'an. <a href="/atlas?year=16&amp;focus=xin-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
